<!-- SECTION 1: Introduction -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Sine Ratio Calculator?</h2>
  <p>A sine ratio calculator answers one question: for an acute angle in a right triangle, how does the side across from the angle compare with the longest side? Enter the opposite side and the hypotenuse, and the tool divides the opposite side by the hypotenuse, returning <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=a/c]]')} />. A right triangle has an opposite side of 3 cm and a hypotenuse of 5 cm, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=3/5=0.6]]')} />. That 0.6 is the sine ratio: it tells you the opposite side is 60 percent of the hypotenuse. It is not an angle, so never read it as <span set:html={rich('[[0.6^\\circ]]')} />. The adjacent side never enters this calculation; the opposite side and the hypotenuse are sufficient on their own. Sine is one of the fundamental trigonometric ratios, and its power is that it compares two lengths in a way that depends only on the angle, not on the size of the triangle. If the angle in degrees is what you need, inverse sine converts the ratio into degrees.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2: Understanding the right triangle -->
<section id="sec-triangle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Opposite Side and Hypotenuse</h2>

  <h3>What Is a Right Triangle?</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has one angle of exactly <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />, the angle between two perpendicular lines, like a wall meeting a level floor. The hypotenuse lies opposite that right angle and is always the longest side, because it must span the full diagonal across the corner. The other two angles share the remaining 90 degrees, so both are acute. The two legs meet at the right angle, perpendicular to each other, and it is their relationship with the hypotenuse that makes right-triangle trigonometry possible.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Opposite Side?</h3>
  <p>The opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the side directly across from the reference angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, the one side that never touches its vertex. In practical problems it often plays the role of a vertical rise, a height difference, or a perpendicular component: the wall height a ladder reaches, the vertical rise of a ramp, or the elevation difference in a survey. Which side counts as opposite depends on the chosen reference angle, so switching from angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> to angle <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> swaps the opposite and adjacent labels. It appears in the numerator of the sine formula because sine measures how large that across-the-angle side is relative to the hypotenuse.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the side opposite the right angle: in any diagram it faces the small square marking the <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} /> corner, and it is strictly longer than either leg. In practical problems it often plays the role of a sloping length: a ladder, a ramp surface, a rafter, a cable, a diagonal brace, or a straight-line path. It appears in the denominator of the sine formula, which is why every sine value comes out between 0 and 1: the numerator is always a genuine leg, shorter than the denominator.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Adjacent Side?</h3>
  <p>The adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the leg that touches the reference angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> without being the hypotenuse. It plays no role in the primary sine calculation, which is why the calculator never asks for it. It becomes useful when calculating cosine, tangent, or a missing side, and when the Pythagorean theorem is used to verify that the two known lengths form a genuine right triangle.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('sine-anatomy.svg')} alt="Right triangle with angle A bottom-left, gold opposite side a, blue hypotenuse c, sin(A)=a/c" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Angle A sits at the bottom-left; sine uses the gold opposite side a and the blue hypotenuse c, not the adjacent side b.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3: What is sine -->
<section id="sec-whatis" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Sine Ratio</h2>
  <p>The sine of an acute angle compares the side directly across from that angle with the longest side of the triangle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)=\\frac{a}{c}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Read <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)]]')} /> as "the sine of angle A". The numerator <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the opposite side; the denominator <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse. The fraction answers a single, concrete question: what portion of the hypotenuse does the opposite side make up? Because both entries are lengths measured in the same unit, the result is a plain number, and that number depends only on the angle, never on the triangle's size.</p>

  <h3>Understanding SOH-CAH-TOA</h3>
  <p>Students remember the three main ratios with the mnemonic SOH-CAH-TOA:</p>
  <p><strong>SOH:</strong> Sine = Opposite / Hypotenuse. <strong>CAH:</strong> Cosine = Adjacent / Hypotenuse. <strong>TOA:</strong> Tangent = Opposite / Adjacent.</p>
  <p>SOH corresponds directly to the sine formula <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=a/c]]')} />. The mnemonic works because it pairs each ratio name with the two sides it needs, in the right order: for sine, the opposite side goes on top and the hypotenuse goes underneath. Once the two sides are named correctly, the mnemonic turns "which side goes where" from a memory problem into a lookup.</p>

  <h3>Why Sine Is a Ratio</h3>
  <p>Dividing two lengths produces a dimensionless number because the units cancel:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\frac{3\\,\\text{m}}{5\\,\\text{m}}=0.6]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The metres cancel, leaving 0.6. Using 300 cm and 500 cm gives the identical result, since both lengths scale together. The only requirement is compatible units: both measurements must use the same unit, or one must be converted before dividing. The ratio is therefore a property of the angle, not of the particular ruler used to measure the triangle.</p>

  <h3>What Does a Sine Value of 0.6 Mean?</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=0.6]]')} /> means the opposite side is 60 percent of the hypotenuse. The 3-4-5 triangle is the classic reference: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=3/5=0.6]]')} />. The number reveals the triangle's shape at a glance: a sine near 1 means the opposite side nearly matches the hypotenuse and the angle is steep; a sine near 0 means the opposite side is short relative to the hypotenuse and the angle is shallow. None of this suggests that 0.6 is an angle; it is a proportion, and only inverse sine turns it into degrees.</p>

  <h3>Why Sine Is Between 0 and 1</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is longer than the opposite side, so <span set:html={rich('[[0 \\lt a \\lt c]]')} />, and therefore:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{0 \\lt \\frac{a}{c} \\lt 1}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>A valid, non-degenerate right triangle cannot have a sine ratio of exactly 0 or exactly 1 for its acute angle. The value approaches 0 as the angle approaches <span set:html={rich('[[0^\\circ]]')} />, because the opposite side shrinks toward nothing; it approaches 1 as the angle approaches <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />, because the opposite side grows to nearly match the hypotenuse. Neither limiting value is attained by the acute angle of a real triangle.</p>

  <h3>Sine Versus Cosine</h3>
  <p>The two ratios share the same hypotenuse but use different numerators:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=a/c]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=b/c]]')} /></p>
  <p>Sine uses the opposite side, the leg across from the angle; cosine uses the adjacent side, the leg next to the angle. A result above 1 means the fraction was flipped or the hypotenuse was placed in the numerator. Sine is not the reciprocal of cosine: the reciprocal of sine is cosecant.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('sine-formula.svg')} alt="Three steps: label a=3, b=4, c=5 on a triangle, apply sin(A)=a/c, get 0.6, meaning 60 percent" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Identify the sides, apply the formula, and calculate: the opposite side is 60 percent of the hypotenuse.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 4: Formula derivation -->
<section id="sec-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How the Sine Formula Works</h2>
  <p>The formula follows a logical sequence. Start with the definition in words:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{\\text{Opposite}}{\\text{Hypotenuse}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Then use the existing page's notation, with <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> for the opposite side and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> for the hypotenuse:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)=\\frac{a}{c}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The opposite side belongs in the numerator because sine measures that side's size relative to the hypotenuse; the hypotenuse belongs in the denominator because it is the reference length every other side is compared against. The arrangement is fixed: numerator asks "how big is this leg?", denominator answers "compared to what?".</p>

  <h3>Fully Worked Example: A 3-4-5 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula. <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=a/c]]')} /></p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the known values. <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=3/5]]')} /></p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Divide. <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)=0.6}]]')} /></p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Interpret the result. The opposite side is 60 percent of the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Verify the triangle. <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=5^2]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[9+16=25]]')} />. The Pythagorean check confirms these side lengths form a right triangle, so the sine result is trustworthy.</p>

  <h3>Important Clarification</h3>
  <p>If the reader wants the angle rather than its sine ratio, inverse sine is required: <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\sin^{-1}(0.6)\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Inverse sine is also called arcsine and is a separate operation from calculating the sine ratio. The existing sine ratio calculator computes the ratio from two side lengths; it does not directly return the angle.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 5: How to use -->
<section id="sec-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Sine Ratio Calculator</h2>
  <p>The calculator mirrors the formula exactly: two inputs, one button, one ratio. Walk through the interface in order.</p>
  <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
    <li><strong>Identify the reference angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />.</strong> Every sine calculation is anchored to one acute angle; the opposite side is defined relative to it.</li>
    <li><strong>Locate the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, directly across from angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />.</strong> It is the leg that never touches the angle's vertex.</li>
    <li><strong>Locate the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, opposite the right angle and the longest side.</strong> It faces the small square marking the <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} /> corner.</li>
    <li><strong>Obtain or measure both side lengths.</strong> Use a tape, a plan dimension, or values from the problem statement.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the opposite-side length in the first input field.</strong> This becomes the numerator <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the hypotenuse length in the second input field.</strong> This becomes the denominator <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Select compatible measurement units using the available unit selectors.</strong> Metres with metres, centimetres with centimetres; mixed units give a meaningless ratio.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm that both measurements are positive.</strong> Zero or negative lengths cannot form a triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Check that the hypotenuse is longer than the opposite side.</strong> If <span set:html={rich('[[a \\ge c]]')} />, the inputs are swapped or the triangle is impossible.</li>
    <li><strong>Click Calculate sin(A).</strong> The tool divides the opposite side by the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the resulting sine ratio.</strong> It is a plain decimal, not an angle and not a length.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm that the result is greater than 0 and less than 1.</strong> For a valid, non-degenerate right triangle with an acute reference angle, that is the only legal range.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Reset when you want to clear the inputs and begin another calculation.</strong></li>
  </ol>
  <p>The calculator does not require the adjacent side or an angle input. It performs the division and nothing else: no saved history, no graphs, no hidden modes.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 6: Foundational examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Examples of Calculating Sine</h2>

  <h3>Example 1: A 3-4-5 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{3}{5}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)=0.6}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The opposite side is 60 percent of the hypotenuse.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: A 5-12-13 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=5\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=13\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{5}{13}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)\\approx0.3846}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The opposite side is approximately 38.46 percent of the hypotenuse.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: A 30-60-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>For a reference angle of <span set:html={rich('[[A=30^\\circ]]')} />, the opposite side is half the hypotenuse. Use <span set:html={rich('[[a=1\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=2\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(30^\\circ)=\\frac{1}{2}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\sin(30^\\circ)=0.5}]]')} /></p>
  <p>This is a useful special-angle result: at 30 degrees the opposite side is exactly half the hypotenuse, which is why this triangle appears in so many textbook problems.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: A 45-45-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=1\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=1\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(45^\\circ)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\sin(45^\\circ)\\approx0.7071}]]')} /></p>
  <p>Equal legs produce equal sine and cosine values for the two acute angles: at 45 degrees each acute angle faces a leg of 1 and a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{2}]]')} />, so sine and cosine coincide.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 7: Practical applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Examples of Using the Sine Ratio</h2>
  <p>Each example states the situation, identifies the opposite side and hypotenuse, substitutes them into the formula, and interprets the result in plain English.</p>

  <h3>Example 5: Calculating a Ladder's Sine Ratio</h3>
  <p>A ladder is 5 m long and reaches 4 m up a vertical wall. The reference angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is between the ladder and the horizontal ground. The ladder is the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the vertical height is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=4\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{4}{5}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)=0.8}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The vertical height is 80 percent of the ladder's length. The ratio describes the ladder's geometry and does not establish whether the ladder is safely positioned.</p>

  <h3>Example 6: Calculating a Roof Rafter's Sine Ratio</h3>
  <p>A roof rafter has a sloping length of 6.5 m and a vertical rise of 2.5 m. The rafter is the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[c=6.5\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the vertical rise is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=2.5\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{2.5}{6.5}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)\\approx0.3846}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The vertical rise is approximately 38.46 percent of the rafter's length. The ratio does not establish structural suitability or roof-design compliance.</p>

  <h3>Example 7: Evaluating a Ramp's Sine Ratio</h3>
  <p>A simplified ramp has a sloping length of 9.5 m and a vertical rise of 0.75 m. The slope is the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[c=9.5\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the vertical rise is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=0.75\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{0.75}{9.5}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)\\approx0.07895}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The vertical rise is approximately 7.89 percent of the sloping length, a gentle slope. This ratio alone does not establish accessibility or building-code compliance.</p>

  <h3>Example 8: A Surveying Measurement</h3>
  <p>A simplified surveying triangle has a straight-line distance of 50 m and a vertical elevation difference of 30 m. The straight line is the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[c=50\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the vertical difference is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=30\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{30}{50}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)=0.6}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The vertical difference is 60 percent of the straight-line distance. Real surveying may require additional corrections, instrument-height adjustments, and more detailed measurements.</p>

  <h3>Example 9: A Guy Wire Supporting a Pole</h3>
  <p>A guy wire is 13 m long, and its attachment point is 5 m above the ground anchor's level. The wire is the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the vertical separation is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=5\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
