  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)\\approx0.3846}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The vertical separation is approximately 38.46 percent of the wire's length. The ratio does not establish wire tension, anchor strength, or structural safety.</p>

  <h3>Example 10: Calculating a Staircase Stringer's Sine Ratio</h3>
  <p>A simplified staircase stringer is 5 m long and spans a vertical rise of 3 m. The stringer is the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the vertical rise is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{3}{5}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)=0.6}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The vertical rise is 60 percent of the diagonal stringer's length. The calculation does not verify staircase dimensions or building-code compliance.</p>

  <h3>Example 11: Understanding a Crane Boom's Vertical Component</h3>
  <p>A simplified crane boom has a length of 10 m and a vertical component of 8 m. The boom is the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[c=10\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the vertical component is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=8\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
