  <h3>Example 14: A Hiking Route's Sine Ratio</h3>
  <p>A simplified straight hiking section has a path length of 100 m and a vertical elevation gain of 25 m. The path is the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[c=100\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the elevation gain is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=25\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{25}{100}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)=0.25}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The elevation gain is 25 percent of the straight path length. Actual hiking trails may contain curves and changing gradients.</p>

  <h3>Example 15: An Awning Support</h3>
  <p>An awning support has a length of 3 m and a vertical component of 1.2 m. The support is the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[c=3\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the vertical component is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=1.2\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{1.2}{3}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)=0.4}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The vertical component is 40 percent of the support's length. The calculation does not establish structural suitability.</p>

  <h3>Example 16: A Line of Sight to the Top of a Tree</h3>
  <p>A simplified line of sight to a tree's top measures 20 m. The vertical difference between the observer's eye level and the tree's top is 12 m. The line of sight is the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[c=20\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the vertical difference is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=12\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{12}{20}]]')} /></p>
