<!-- SECTION 8: Angle relationship -->
<section id="sec-angle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Relationship Between Sine and Angle Size</h2>
  <p>Sine increases as the reference angle increases. The geometry explains why: when angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is small, the opposite side is short relative to the hypotenuse, so the ratio sits near 0. As the angle widens, the opposite side becomes a larger share of the hypotenuse, and the ratio climbs toward 1. At <span set:html={rich('[[30^\\circ]]')} /> the sine is exactly 0.5; at <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} /> it is about 0.7071; at <span set:html={rich('[[60^\\circ]]')} /> about 0.8660; and as the angle approaches <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />, sine approaches 1.</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /></th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Sine ratio</th>
          <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-on-surface">Interpretation</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[30^\\circ]]')} /></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">0.5</td><td class="py-2.5">Opposite side is half the hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} /></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Approximately 0.7071</td><td class="py-2.5">Opposite side is about 70.71% of the hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[60^\\circ]]')} /></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Approximately 0.8660</td><td class="py-2.5">Opposite side is about 86.60% of the hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[80^\\circ]]')} /></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Approximately 0.9848</td><td class="py-2.5">Opposite side is about 98.48% of the hypotenuse</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The pattern is simple and monotonic: every increase in the angle raises the sine, and the ratio stays between 0 and 1 for every acute angle. Sine describes the triangle's shape rather than its absolute size: two triangles with the same acute angle have the same sine ratio even when their side lengths differ. A <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} /> entry is a limiting reference value, not an acute angle in a non-degenerate right triangle.</p>

