<!-- Conclusion -->
<section id="sec-conclusion" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Use the Sine Ratio With Confidence</h2>
  <p>Sine is the first of the three main ratios: divide the opposite side by the hypotenuse and you have described what portion of the longest side sits across from the angle. The formula <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=a/c]]')} /> needs only two measurements, the answer is always a dimensionless number between 0 and 1, and it grows as the reference angle grows. When a missing side is needed, <span set:html={rich('[[a=c\\sin(A)]]')} /> recovers the opposite side; when the angle is needed, <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\sin^{-1}(a/c)]]')} /> recovers it. Ladder, rafter, ramp, or textbook 3-4-5, the method never changes: identify the opposite side, identify the hypotenuse, divide, and verify with the 0-to-1 range.</p>
</section>
